ifficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17b28500-a29b-5fb9-91ad-6adad54b2535" :question/math-academy-id "q-39992" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set \n${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0368bac0-984e-5787-80ab-57f2797279e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324006} {:answer/id #uuid "146ade24-131c-5a2f-b98f-ec8b390c591c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324156} {:answer/id #uuid "8957b4dc-3288-5bc8-94b9-4b3ea2e960ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325143} {:answer/id #uuid "98e891e2-50be-5dde-9dc9-f9157bf702d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325271} {:answer/id #uuid "ab03c3c3-e63d-51f9-b995-161f52c4e24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab03c3c3-e63d-51f9-b995-161f52c4e24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325426} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f98642b-b39d-56b1-9d79-30da4adf0483" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e6b99aa-c1fb-5c62-9a05-f09dd90462a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-40616" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{20\\cdot 4+(-20)\\cdot 5+0\\cdot 0}{{4}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=-\\frac{20}{41}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{20\\cdot 5+(-20)\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 0}{{5}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{180}{41}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{41}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{180}{41}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{41}(-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The projection is the vector $x$ itself. This means that $x$ can be written as a linear combination of ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ In other words, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4920a399-1a1b-594a-9001-b155df82b39e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394183} {:answer/id #uuid "24bed580-c0bb-5067-92b0-4c041de3af13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394184} {:answer/id #uuid "5c66e44d-7386-5598-9c1a-fddcb60c5216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394185} {:answer/id #uuid "45cbe354-7fbf-580c-bfde-cd07d3eee1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394186} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cffea4-3a62-5b3f-abc2-6629aef59007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4920a399-1a1b-594a-9001-b155df82b39e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394183} :answer-field/id #uuid "df20fd30-d10b-5edc-90eb-9e19ad4dd340" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ea59652-3e77-5eac-9804-089948d56101" :question/math-academy-id "q-81475" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+(-17)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=-\\frac{24}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4\\cdot 3+6\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 0+(-17)\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{35}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{4\\cdot 11+6\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 0+(-17)\\cdot (-8)}{{11}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-8)^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{210}{210}[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e1142b8-71d3-5bab-adfc-60cf92d5318c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-13 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348567} {:answer/id #uuid "250736d9-adff-5854-90e2-dd99ffdcb9d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-13 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348765} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2f26ae-b2f0-52d9-b035-6b337eb24c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349021} {:answer/id #uuid "70221197-9d1a-5f4f-bd40-f730db4ecb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349478} {:answer/id #uuid "8c121211-47bd-5e10-9008-c874688472a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e1142b8-71d3-5bab-adfc-60cf92d5318c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-13 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348567} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9cbfe52-8cc0-5200-b490-45b7c6bbce15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bcdeb99-3d44-592c-ac68-43915fe5ae89" :question/math-academy-id "q-81593" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 31\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-4)+31\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}}v=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 2+31\\cdot (-2)}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}w=-5[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-13 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
